        content: [
          { t: 'p', text: 'Some days the entire market rises or falls together, every sector at once. That is rarely about any company — it is **macro**: a scheduled economic number surprised the crowd. You already met the machinery (interest rates and the cycle, in Financial Concepts). This lesson is the field guide to the actual calendar.' },
          { t: 'h', text: 'The big five releases' },
          { t: 'table', headers: ['Release', 'What it measures', 'Why markets care'], rows: [
            ['Inflation (CPI)', 'Price changes for a basket of goods', 'Drives central-bank rate decisions — the discount rate on every asset'],
            ['Jobs report', 'Hiring, unemployment, wage growth', 'The economy\'s pulse; also feeds inflation via wages'],
            ['Central bank decision', 'The policy interest rate, and the statement', 'Directly sets the "price of money"; the *wording* about future moves matters as much as the rate'],
            ['GDP', 'Total economic output', 'The scoreboard — but backward-looking and heavily revised'],
            ['PMI surveys', 'Purchasing managers: expanding or contracting?', 'Early warning — survey data leads the hard numbers'],
          ]},
          { t: 'h', text: 'The expectations game, again' },
          { t: 'p', text: 'Exactly like earnings: markets move on **surprise, not level**. Inflation at 4% can trigger a rally if 4.5% was feared, and a sell-off if 3.5% was hoped for. This produces the famous backwards days — "good news is bad news" — when a strong jobs report *sinks* stocks because it implies rates stay higher for longer. The chain to memorise: **data → expected rate path → discount rates → stock prices.**' },
          { t: 'callout', warn: true, title: 'What to do with all this', text: 'For an investor: almost nothing, deliberately. Track the regime — is inflation trending up or down, are rates rising or falling — because it explains *why* your whole portfolio moved and which sectors face headwinds. But do not trade the releases. You are competing with people who parse the text in microseconds, and being 30 seconds late to a CPI print is the same as being wrong.' },
          { t: 'p', text: 'The practical habit: know when the big releases land (any economic calendar lists them), expect index-wide volatility those mornings, and never mistake a macro day for a verdict on your company. When everything falls 2% together, your stock did not fail — the discount rate moved.' },
        ],
